#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188149 :question/id #uuid "da25ceb7-d74a-57be-a183-3bc0e0677871" :question/math-academy-id "e-13546" :question/problem "Compute the value of $3,200 \\div 4$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $3,200$ is the same as $32$ hundreds. So, we have\n\n$$\n3,200 = 32 \\times {100}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3, 200 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 32 \\cdot 100 \\div 4 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32 \\cdot 100 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n32 \\div 4 \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n(32 \\div 4) \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n&= 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3,200\\div 4 = 800\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de64fc7-fb93-5d22-8f16-5303159ac96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475481} {:answer/id #uuid "2112bd3d-e542-5085-90cd-bcbd60ea2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475485} {:answer/id #uuid "2f24c363-f085-58b6-847a-2afc26d5f4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475506} {:answer/id #uuid "3d790aef-7b50-510a-afd1-29b89543cb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475530} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb211f6-50dd-5038-b436-55be68b355ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f24c363-f085-58b6-847a-2afc26d5f4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-2h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8941c1b-9cc5-546c-9b11-85c5048affec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c5debf-9545-5811-9ea3-88835b4357f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144142" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $4-(2h-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n4-2h+3\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2h+3 & = \\\\ (4+3)-2h & = \\\\ 7-2h & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "503a5a0c-6d91-57c6-948d-dc1e42b752c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504177} {:answer/id #uuid "7896240f-c609-59c4-ad0e-5e18c1ac5585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504238} {:answer/id #uuid "e47bba09-a7c0-55d0-b09a-a6c628a5c61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504426} {:answer/id #uuid "f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504458} {:answer/id #uuid "f78366f5-867d-50a1-aae2-976f8223d54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504458} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c1fbb4-4134-53c6-a8fa-ae4d00ea2050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb908c23-fe0f-52b8-bd9d-1f4c58233cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-57060" :question/problem "$5-3(x+1)=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $5$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n5-[3(x+1)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n5-[3x+3].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n5-3x-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3x-3 & = \\\\ (5-3)-3x & = \\\\ 2-3x & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192431 :question/id #uuid "1e4156ce-9dea-599c-b589-fe29fe23e110" :question/math-academy-id "e-10589" :question/problem "A wheel has $18$ spokes. The angles between the adjacent spokes are identical. Find the measure of the shaded angle.\n\n![](images/4c/4c284f9277032944699f42b7ed022fc31d3b6f72fc3ce5cf0c07233f3c8de89d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The spokes of the wheel form $18$ identical angles. Since a full angle has a measure of $360^\\circ$, the measure of one division is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{18}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the value of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{360}{18} &=  \\\\\n360 \\div 18 &=  \\\\\n(36 \\div 18) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{18} = 20^\\circ\n$$\n\nThe shaded angle consists of $4$ divisions, so its measure is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 20^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192434 :question/id #uuid "415295e3-8f7a-581f-8433-33b10c3faba4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10593" :question/problem "![](images/54/54f117c57d1c3bb1db17085a74ac83bb8304ddd8c84bb5f213c0c4456b1f5a53.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle{AOB}$ is $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ of a full turn. What is the degree measure of the angle?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{60} \\cdot 360^{∘} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{60} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{360^{∘}}{60} &=  \\\\\n&= (\\frac{360}{60})^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this division problem, we first divide the numerator and denominator by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360}{60}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360 \\div  10}{60 \\div  10}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{36 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0}}{6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{36}{6}} & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the fraction by expressing it as a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{36}{6} &=  \\\\\n36 \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is $6^\\circ$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192435 :question/id #uuid "29e61984-9a23-54bd-b7dc-175f3958977d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10601" :question/problem "![](images/37/37e229d14775daf5cb2958010ba8be408bd84256661457ea0dd255195aaf1960.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle{AOB}$ is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of a full turn. Expressed as an improper fraction, what is the degree measure of the angle?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 360^{∘} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{12} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^{∘}}{12} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's common to write the fraction in parentheses and place the degree symbol outside, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{360}{12})^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192446 :question/id #uuid "463a3dd9-d9ba-5cf2-89ec-9d1c27a16163" :question/math-academy-id "e-10644" :question/problem "Express $\\left(3 \\times 10^{-9} \\right) - \\left(6 \\times 10^{-10} \\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot 10^{-10} &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10^{-9 - 1} &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10^{-1 - 9} &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-9} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.6 \\cdot 10^{-9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 \\cdot 10^{-9}) - (6 \\cdot 10^{-10}) &=  \\\\\n(3 \\cdot 10^{-9}) - (0.6 \\cdot 10^{-9}) &=  \\\\\n(3 - 0.6) \\cdot 10^{-9} &=  \\\\\n&= 2.4 \\cdot 10^{-9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192447 :question/id #uuid "b48cba18-1218-5058-bb6a-6b1b3121b004" :question/math-academy-id "e-10645" :question/problem "Express $\\left(8 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) + \\left(9.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The first number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the second number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 \\cdot 10^{-4} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{-3 - 1} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{-1 - 3} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.8 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(8 \\cdot 10^{-4}) + (9.5 \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n(0.8 \\cdot 10^{-3}) + (9.5 \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n(0.8 + 9.5) \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 10.3 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above number is not in scientific notation because $10.3 \\gt 10$. So, we need to write $10.3$ in scientific notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n10.3 = 1.03\\times 10^{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10.3 \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n(1.03 \\cdot 10^{1}) \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n1.03 \\cdot (10^{1} \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n1.03 \\cdot 10^{1 - 3} &=  \\\\\n&= 1.03 \\cdot 10^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $1.03 \\times 10^{-2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192448 :question/id #uuid "00e8f1d5-92f5-5b97-8da7-ed258385dfa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10646" :question/problem "The maximum acceptable concentration for nitrate in drinking water is $1 \\times 10^{-3} \\, \\mathrm{g/L}$.\nLaboratory analysis established that the concentration of nitrate in the water of a particular mountain river is $7 \\times 10^{-4} \\, \\mathrm{g/L}$ less than the maximum acceptable concentration for drinking water. What is the concentration of nitrate in this river?" :question/worked-solution "We need to write the following expression in scientific notation:\n$1 \\times 10^{-3} - 7 \\times 10^{-4}$.\n\nThe second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 \\cdot 10^{-4} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-3 - 1} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-1 - 3} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.7 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 \\cdot 10^{-3}) - (7 \\cdot 10^{-4}) &=  \\\\\n(1 \\cdot 10^{-3}) - (0.7 \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n(1 - 0.7) \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.3 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above number is not in scientific notation because $0.3 \\lt 1$. So, we need to write $0.3$ in scientific notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3 = 3\\times 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3 \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n(3 \\cdot 10^{-1}) \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot (10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 10^{-4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the concentration of nitrate in the river is $3 \\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{g/L}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192450 :question/id #uuid "519b0ae6-b372-5278-b97c-0e9b4bf5d217" :question/math-academy-id "e-10659" :question/problem "Express $\\left(6.2 \\times 10^{13} \\right) - \\left(8.1 \\times 10^{12} \\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8.1 \\cdot 10^{12} &=  \\\\\n8.1 \\cdot 10^{13 - 1} &=  \\\\\n8.1 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 13} &=  \\\\\n8.1 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{13} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.81 \\cdot 10^{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6.2 \\cdot 10^{13}) - (8.1 \\cdot 10^{12}) &=  \\\\\n(6.2 \\cdot 10^{13}) - (0.81 \\cdot 10^{13}) &=  \\\\\n(6.2 - 0.81) \\cdot 10^{13} &=  \\\\\n&= 5.39 \\cdot 10^{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192451 :question/id #uuid "c37984c4-1ee4-5d0b-8a26-1ba0c7d643b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10660" :question/problem "Express $\\left(7 \\times 10^{7}\\right) - \\left(1 \\times 10^{8}\\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The first number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the second number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 \\cdot 10^{7} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{8 - 1} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 8} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{8} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.7 \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(7 \\cdot 10^{7}) - (1 \\cdot 10^{8}) &=  \\\\\n(0.7 \\cdot 10^{8}) - (1 \\cdot 10^{8}) &=  \\\\\n(0.7 - 1) \\cdot 10^{8} &=  \\\\\n&= - 0.3 \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above number is not in scientific notation because $0.3 < 1$. So, we need to write $0.3$ in scientific notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3 = 3\\times 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-0.3 \\cdot 10^{8} &= - (3 \\cdot 10^{-1}) \\cdot 10^{8} \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot (10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{8}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 8} \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 10^{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $-3 \\times 10^{7}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192453 :question/id #uuid "5c37f1f8-3db9-5571-bf5b-36b80f758a92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10681" :question/problem "Express $\\left(4 \\times 10^{5} \\right) - \\left(8 \\times 10^{3}\\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 \\cdot 10^{3} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{5 - 2} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{-2 + 5} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10^{-2} \\cdot 10^{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.08 \\cdot 10^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4 \\cdot 10^{5}) - (8 \\cdot 10^{3}) &=  \\\\\n(4 \\cdot 10^{5}) - (0.08 \\cdot 10^{5}) &=  \\\\\n(4 - 0.08) \\cdot 10^{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 3.92 \\cdot 10^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192454 :question/id #uuid "b2e1dc89-46d9-5a18-a6cf-6fe08985e35d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10684" :question/problem "The flow rate of the Amazon River is $2.8 \\times 10^{8}$ liters per second, while the flow rate of the Orinoco River is $3.3 \\times 10^{7}$ liters per second. How much greater is the flow in the Amazon river compared to the Orinoco river?" :question/worked-solution "We need to write the following expression in scientific notation:\n$2.8 \\times 10^{8} - 3.3 \\times 10^{7}$.\n\nThe second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.3 \\cdot 10^{7} &=  \\\\\n3.3 \\cdot 10^{8 - 1} &=  \\\\\n3.3 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 8} &=  \\\\\n3.3 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{8} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.33 \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the subtraction, we get\n$(2.8 \\cdot 10^{8}) - (3.3 \\cdot 10^{7}); (2.8 \\cdot 10^{8}) - (0.33 \\cdot 10^{8}); (2.8 - 0.33) \\cdot 10^{8}; 2.47 \\cdot 10^{8}$.\n\nTherefore, the Amazon river flow is $2.47 \\times 10^{8}$ liters per second more than the Orinoco."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192458 :question/id #uuid "fa8b0f38-fbf5-58c2-b35a-d77301bfd3b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10720" :question/problem "Round $679 \\div 6$ to the nearest whole number." :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $1$$1$$3$$6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$${1}$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n679 \\div 6 = 113 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nWriting this as a mixed number, we obtain\n\n$$\n679 \\div 6 = 113 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}} < \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe need to round down, as follows:\n\n$$\n113 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}} \\to 113\n$$\n\nTherefore, $679 \\div 6$ rounded to the nearest whole number is $113$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192459 :question/id #uuid "5defbab3-6425-54aa-8493-a4cc716b2b57" :question/math-academy-id "e-10721" :question/problem "Round $4,444 \\div 7$ to the nearest whole number." :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$3$$4$$7$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$2$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$3$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$6$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n4,444 \\div 7 = 634 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 6\n$$\n\nWriting this as a mixed number, we obtain\n\n$$\n4,444 \\div 7 = 634\\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{6}{7}}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{6}{7}} > \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe need to round up, as follows:\n\n$$\n634 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{6}{7}} \\to 635\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4,444 \\div 7$ rounded to the nearest whole number is $635$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192463 :question/id #uuid "d43eec5a-77b9-5e99-85a6-32bb2447e9fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10743" :question/problem "Round $83 \\div 5$ to the nearest whole number." :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$3$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$3$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n83 \\div 5 = 16 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3\n$$\n\nWriting this as a mixed number, we obtain\n\n$$\n83 \\div 5 = 16 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{5}}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{5}} > \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe need to round up, as follows:\n\n$$\n16 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{5}} \\to 17\n$$\n\nTherefore, $83 \\div 5$ rounded to the nearest whole number is $17$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192497 :question/id #uuid "5a5f58f1-da16-5757-bc0f-8c8fbc34303c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10960" :question/problem "How many yards are in $168$ inches?" :question/worked-solution "To convert $168$ inches into yards, we convert $168$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $168$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $168$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $168{:}$\n$168 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 168 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{168}{12}ft|= 168in$\nNow, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{168}{12}$ as the division $168 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n$1$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$\nTherefore, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n\n- Next, to convert from $14$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $14$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $17{:}$\n$14 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 14 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{14}{3}yd|= 14ft; 4\\frac{2}{3}yd|= 14ft$\nTherefore, $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192536 :question/id #uuid "769bff46-ac3a-5b75-93f5-5f8f9fa47554" :question/math-academy-id "e-12842" :question/problem "Evaluate $5 \\times 4 -7$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 4 -7 = ({\\color{blue}5 \\times 4})-7\n$$\n\nWe carry out the operations inside the parentheses first. We have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5 \\times 4} = {\\color{blue}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5 \\cdot 4) - 7 &=  \\\\\n20 - 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 13 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192537 :question/id #uuid "1a0076ce-bc17-5398-a430-47149b04927c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12843" :question/problem "Evaluate $4 + 10 \\times 2$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n$$\n4+ 10 \\times 2 = 4 + ({\\color{blue}10 \\times 2})\n$$\n\nWe carry out the operations inside the parentheses first. We have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}10 \\times 2} = {\\color{blue}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 + (10 \\cdot 2) &=  \\\\\n4 + 20 &=  \\\\\n&= 24 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192538 :question/id #uuid "0c43c7a9-a277-51e1-a08a-f646c5a67f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12844" :question/problem "Evaluate $3 \\times 9 - 5 \\times 5$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 9 - 5 \\times 5 = ({\\color{blue}3 \\times 9}) - ({\\color{red}5 \\times 5})\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 9 &= 27 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 5 &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 \\cdot 9) - (5 \\cdot 5) &=  \\\\\n27 - 25 &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192545 :question/id #uuid "bab434ec-d18a-5d77-9bf1-8e16e9af2556" :question/math-academy-id "e-12974" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4y -1 > \\dfrac {5y - 2} {3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-1 & >\\frac{5y-2}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >3\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{3}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >\\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{\\cancel{3}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >5y-2 \\\\ 12y-3 & >5y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t12y-3\t&> 5y-2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t12y-3-5y\t&> 5y-2-5y \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3\t&> {-2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3 +3\t&> {-2} +3 \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y\t\t&> {1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{7y}{7}\t&> \\dfrac{1}7 \\[5pt]\n\t\ty\t\t&> \\dfrac{1}{7}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y > \\dfrac17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192552 :question/id #uuid "1a6542a3-a488-59fd-9f35-bdb542f3f849" :question/math-academy-id "e-13402" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the equation below?\n\n$2$" :question/worked-solution "We can *borrow* $1$ from the hundreds place and convert it to tens.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n[math]2[/math] hundreds + [math]1[/math] ten + [math]1[/math] one = ([math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] hundred) + [math]1[/math] ten + [math]1[/math] one\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $1$ hundred into $10$ tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n([math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] hundred) + [math]1[/math] ten + [math]1[/math] one &=  \\\\\n([math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}10[/math] tens) + [math]1[/math] ten + [math]1[/math] one &=  \\\\\n[math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + ([math]\\color{blue}10[/math] tens + [math]1[/math] ten) + [math]1[/math] one &=  \\\\\n&= [math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}11[/math] tens + [math]1[/math] one\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192608 :question/id #uuid "1202597b-093e-5750-9307-9cd97ff281b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2085" :question/problem "How many yards are in $63$ feet?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the unit conversion between yards and feet:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{yd} = 3 \\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from feet to yards, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 \\,\\textrm{yd} = 1 \\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $63$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $63{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n63 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd &= 63 \\cdot 1ft \\\\\n\\frac{63}{3}yd &= 63ft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{63}{3}\n$$\n\nas the division $63 \\div 3$.\nWe can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  | $2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$ |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $3$ |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ |  | $3$ |\n|  |  | $\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nTherefore, $63\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $21\\,\\textrm{yd}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192611 :question/id #uuid "318c41b2-bbb0-5752-a012-4981dbaa0f35" :question/math-academy-id "e-2096" :question/problem "How many days are in $408$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the unit conversion between days and hours:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{day} = 24 \\, \\textrm{hours}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $24$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{24} \\, \\textrm{days} = 1 \\, \\textrm{hour}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many days are in $408$ hours. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{24}days &= 408 \\cdot 1hour \\\\\n\\frac{408}{24}days &= 408hours\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{408}{24}\n$$\n\nusing long division.\n\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $24$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n|  |  | $\\phantom{0}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $408$ hours equals $17$ days."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203683 :question/id #uuid "7d7f9634-b8b1-5fb8-8f2a-9ecf32699231" :question/math-academy-id "e-14594" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{\\mathbb{Z}, \\mathbb{Q}, \\mathbb{R}\\big\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\:R\\: Y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq X \\cap Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{Z} \\: R \\: \\mathbb{Q}$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{R} \\: R \\: \\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{Q} \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!}\\: \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $x \\: \\require{cancel}\\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X \\: R \\: Y$ if and only if $\\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq X \\cap Y.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have $\\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\cap \\mathbb{Q},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $\\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\cap \\mathbb{R},$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $\\mathbb{Q} \\not\\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\cap \\mathbb{R},$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203695 :question/id #uuid "1488c9fd-0fbf-57b6-bed5-3d060cf87a00" :question/math-academy-id "e-14634" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{\\mathbb{R}_0^+, \\mathbb{R}_0^-, \\mathbb{R}\\big\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\:R\\: Y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad X\\supset Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R} \\: R\\: \\mathbb{R}_0^-$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{R}_0^- \\: R\\: \\mathbb{R}_0^+$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{R}_0^+ \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $x \\: \\require{cancel}\\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X \\: R \\: Y$ if and only if $X\\supset Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\supset$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\not\\supset$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\supset,$ we have $\\mathbb{R} \\supset \\mathbb{R}_0^-.$ This is equivalent to $\\mathbb{R}_0^- \\subset \\mathbb{R},$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\supset,$ we have $\\mathbb{R}_0^- \\supset \\mathbb{R}_0^+.$ This is equivalent to $\\mathbb{R}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{R}_0^-,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ with the symbol $\\not\\supset,$ we have $\\mathbb{R}_0^+ \\not\\supset \\mathbb{R}.$ This is equivalent to $\\mathbb{R} \\not\\subset \\mathbb{R}_0^+,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203744 :question/id #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" :question/math-academy-id "e-15071" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-4w^2-30w-50.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-4w^2-30w-50\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n-2(2w^2+15w+25)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $2w^2+15w+25$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 25 = 50$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $50.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $50$ are\n\n$$\n(1,50),\\quad (2,25),\\quad (5,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,10)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n5+10 =15  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5w+10w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+15w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+5w+10w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+10w+5w+25 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+10w)+(5w+25) & = \\\\ 2w(w+5)+5(w+5) & = \\\\ (2w+5)(w+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2w+5)(w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203745 :question/id #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15088" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-10t^2 - 35t +20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2{t}^{2}+7t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+7t-4 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+8t-4 & = \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-t)+(8t-4) & = \\\\ t(2t-1)+4(2t-1) & = \\\\ (2t-1)(t+4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2t-1)(t+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203746 :question/id #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" :question/math-academy-id "e-15089" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3{x}^{2}-8x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$ \n\n$$\n-2-6= -8  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-8x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-6x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x(x-2)-2(x-2) & = \\\\ (3x-2)(x-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3x-2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330ab0dd-02fd-56db-b804-f8f33e042f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x+4)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507669} {:answer/id #uuid "49cdacc0-415a-5700-bc99-a9255a76dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(4x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507710} {:answer/id #uuid "8072f33f-7cd1-5feb-ae96-8d19e31a9b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(2x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507791} {:answer/id #uuid "81aa656d-a1b1-57e7-9c2a-b9c71123e89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+4)(3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507792} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ab955-2370-54a3-8338-4e9edcb85b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81aa656d-a1b1-57e7-9c2a-b9c71123e89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+4)(3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52abdf3-c0dc-5ef1-894f-dee194666a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a1a0c7d-b300-5f97-894e-7b4ae6893dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-225002" :question/problem "Factor $6{x}^{2}+26x+8.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+26x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{x}^{2}+13x+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{x}^{2}+13x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 4=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(3,4),\\,(6,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,12)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n1+12=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $x+12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+13x+4= \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x+12x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+x)+(12x+4) & = \\\\ x(3x+1)+4(3x+1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(3x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b8e1a5-5367-508e-ac8f-3841576356f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13b8e1a5-5367-508e-ac8f-3841576356f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507621} :answer-field/id #uuid "90c7b183-e533-502b-8176-8388d1565702" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20d43fc-e518-5c12-b7c1-9f2ff9d029af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a20d43fc-e518-5c12-b7c1-9f2ff9d029af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507828} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c3c1df-a7e2-5642-ad06-78f1c0578359" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95cbedfb-d850-5f9f-8cf1-3a3819b2c147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95cbedfb-d850-5f9f-8cf1-3a3819b2c147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507809} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af37f00-7de9-5a1f-b9ee-080fd67e4675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98b7e6f2-e40d-535e-a12b-3e5bd59b4957" :question/math-academy-id "q-225073" :question/problem "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$8{y}^{2}-36y+40=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$y-5)(y\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n8{y}^{2}-36y+40\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $4.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{y}^{2}-9y+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times 10=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-9$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-5)$ add to $-9:$ \n\n$$\n-4-5=-9\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4y-5y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-9y+10 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y-5y+10 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-4y)-(5y-10) & = \\\\ 2y(y-2)-5(y-2) & = \\\\ (2y-5)(y-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}-36y+40 & = \\\\ 4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10) & = \\\\ 4(2y-5)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212794 :question/id #uuid "659e7fd3-3502-5a47-a54c-3d0c33150d73" :question/math-academy-id "e-18928" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{1}{3-\\sqrt a}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $3+\\sqrt a$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $3+\\sqrt a$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{3 + \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/((3 - \\sqrt{a})(3 + \\sqrt{a})) &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/(3^{2} - (\\sqrt{a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{9 - a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212820 :question/id #uuid "33cc9ad9-0ba8-5f65-adb6-41b05eccfc3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-18987" :question/problem "Factor the expression $y^2-\\dfrac{1}{10}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n10 = \\big(\\sqrt{10}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - \\frac{1}{10} &=  \\\\\ny^{2} - (1)/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= y^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2} = (y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212834 :question/id #uuid "f24b62ac-2dee-547f-86c7-4f0ac3f1057e" :question/math-academy-id "e-19007" :question/problem "Factor the following expression:\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(12-z)^2$" :question/worked-solution "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(12-z)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(z-12)$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(12-z)$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n12-z = -(z-12)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(12-z)^2 = (z-12)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(z-12)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-12)\\left[(z+3) + 4(z-12)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - 12)[(z + 3) + 4(z - 12)] &=  \\\\\n(z - 12)[z + 3 + 4z - 48] &=  \\\\\n&= (z - 12)(5z - 45)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212835 :question/id #uuid "8163c2ef-1fdf-532b-9600-22860d3ad041" :question/math-academy-id "e-19008" :question/problem "Consider the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThis expression can be written in the form\n$(2a-7)^2\\cdot g(a)$.\nFind the polynomial $g(a)$." :question/worked-solution "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}\\underbrace{(2a^{2} - 10)}_{g(a)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212849 :question/id #uuid "11d3b3ea-12ee-5d4a-b66b-0f312638e31f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19023" :question/problem "Factor the expression $\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36$." :question/worked-solution "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2} - 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212850 :question/id #uuid "c2ba3085-2a74-5128-83c2-77bbd9750aea" :question/math-academy-id "e-19024" :question/problem "Factor the expression $-5r^2+ 36s^2$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n5 = \\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5r^{2} + 36s^{2} &=  \\\\\n36s^{2} - 5r^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (6s)^{2} - (\\sqrt{5}r)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(6s)^{2} - (\\sqrt{5}r)^{2} = (6s - \\sqrt{5}r)(6s + \\sqrt{5}r)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212963 :question/id #uuid "c74a39cf-7bb8-57aa-a1a5-653e761a2acf" :question/math-academy-id "e-19263" :question/problem "Factor the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)$" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to factor the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(5y-3)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3)\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract the numbers inside the first brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{2}{10})(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 - 2}{10}(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}(5y - 3) \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb8232e-74ec-5125-9023-35f18e70215d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}+4)}{x+18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520859} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd21317-9259-5e13-8f0c-addab41d0918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-2}-4)}{x-18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520865} {:answer/id #uuid "6bed1864-1f50-54e3-8cde-8a57d07fe2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x-14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521008} {:answer/id #uuid "c775aed0-24c4-56d0-8bf0-fec61f178576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x-14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521206} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7bc214-ee6d-5abf-966b-772d87f7e378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\sqrt{x-2}+4}{x-14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c775aed0-24c4-56d0-8bf0-fec61f178576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x-14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521206} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b0ffea-cecd-5abf-8980-0e708de53956" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25d93f27-b081-516b-bfe5-d84f1819ebc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-34502" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+2}-4.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+2}-4,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-4}{\\sqrt{x+2}-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2}+4)(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x+2-16} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x-14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216982 :question/id #uuid "78677c71-50ed-53e9-b1ba-07742eb90c5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-20938" :question/problem "In their last $5$ baseball games, Team $B$ scored a mean of $5$ home runs per game. The number of home runs scored by Team $B$ in each game is given below:\n$7, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 4, \\: 1$\n\nOn the other hand, Team $A$ completed their last five games with a mean absolute deviation (MAD) of $1.5$ home runs.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The MAD of Team $B$'s scores was $1$ home run.\n2. A comparison of the MADs suggests Team $A$'s home run scores had a smaller spread than Team $B$'s.\n3. A comparison of the MADs suggests Team $A$'s home run scores had the same spread as Team $B$'s." :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Team B's scores.\n\nSince we are given Team B's mean (${\\color{blue}5}$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}7 - 5 & + & 5 - 5 & + & 8 - 5 & + & 4 - 5 & + & 1 - 5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & + & 0 & + & 3 & + & -1 & + & -4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n2 + 0 + 3 + 1 + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{5} = 2\n$$\n\nSo, Team B's scores have a mean absolute deviation of $2$ home runs.\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $1.5 < 2$, Team $A$'s home run scores had a smaller spread than Team $B$'s.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224865 :question/id #uuid "0c97c33d-8e28-5096-b798-0e4971fd7785" :question/math-academy-id "e-19884" :question/problem "Factor out the greatest common factor and simplify the expression $48 s^{4} r^{2}+60 s^{2} r$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the greatest common factor $(\\textrm{GCF})$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(48s^4r^2,\\ 60s^2r) = 12s^2r\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite each term as a product involving the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48s^{4}r^{2} + 60s^{2}r &=  \\\\\n&= (12s^{2}r \\cdot 4s^{2}r) + (12s^{2}r \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor out the $\\textrm{GCF}$ using the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12s^{2}r \\cdot 4s^{2}r) + (12s^{2}r \\cdot 5) &=  \\\\\n&= 12s^{2}r(4s^{2}r + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $48 s^{4} r^{2}+60 s^{2} r$ is equivalent to $12rs^2(4rs^2 + 5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231290 :question/id #uuid "aea13ce3-4614-55ab-ae03-0088b5e3c447" :question/math-academy-id "e-21850" :question/problem "$$\n10x^2y'' + 7xy' + (3x-1)y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = -\\dfrac{3a_{n-1}}{f(n)}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n})]{x}^{\\lambda +n} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(5\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we take $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for $n \\geq 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ (2(\\lambda +n)-1)(5(\\lambda +n)+1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda=\\dfrac12$ into the above and solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2(\\frac{1}{2}+n)-1)(5(\\frac{1}{2}+n)+1){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ 2n(5n+\\frac{7}{2}){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(10n+7){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{n(10n+7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=n(10n+7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231320 :question/id #uuid "dc812edf-4737-575a-937b-89bc2af88c38" :question/math-academy-id "e-21963" :question/problem "If $r=\\dfrac57$, then $r^2=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $r$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{7})^{2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5}{7} \\cdot \\frac{5}{7} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 5}{7 \\cdot 7} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{49} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231321 :question/id #uuid "ea250af1-a40c-5833-89dc-2df124e0dd71" :question/math-academy-id "e-21964" :question/problem "If $p=\\dfrac34$, then $4p=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $p$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4p &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot p &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{12}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231322 :question/id #uuid "e02d2e62-fedb-500d-8416-07e4a1e71e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21965" :question/problem "If $k = 30$, then $\\dfrac{k}5 - 3 =$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $k$ with $30$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{5} - 3 &=  \\\\\nk \\div 5 - 3 &=  \\\\\n30 \\div 5 - 3 &=  \\\\\n6 - 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231323 :question/id #uuid "59443451-978e-5ced-80e3-8dbb5aa63fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21966" :question/problem "The distance (in kilometers) that a delivery drone travels after $t$ hours is given by the expression\n$14 - 3t$.\nWhat distance does the drone travel after $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the distance after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours, we replace $t$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 3t &=  \\\\\n14 - 3 \\cdot t &=  \\\\\n14 - \\frac{3}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &=  \\\\\n14 - \\frac{15}{2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{28}{2} - \\frac{15}{2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{2} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance the drone travels after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{13}{2}}\n$$\n\nkilometers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231324 :question/id #uuid "5027578e-0ec2-5db5-8516-7a8d063b51a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21968" :question/problem "If $k=4$, then $3k^2=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $k$ with $4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot k^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 4^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 16 &=  \\\\\n&= 48 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231325 :question/id #uuid "6f50924b-630b-5d74-a6ba-8becf3ce8408" :question/math-academy-id "e-21969" :question/problem "If $n=4$, then $3n^2 + 5=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $n$ with $4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3n^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot n^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 4^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 16 + 5 &=  \\\\\n48 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 53 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231326 :question/id #uuid "8df55194-b24b-54ad-b6cb-1ec76c833210" :question/math-academy-id "e-21970" :question/problem "The amount of paint (in liters) used to cover a wall is given by the expression $\\dfrac12w^2+\\dfrac34$, where $w$ is the width of the wall in meters. How much paint is used when the width of the wall is $\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine how much paint is used when the width of the wall is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe evaluate the expression at\n\n$$\nw=\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nFirst, replacing $w$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nin the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, evaluating the resulting expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{24}{32} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{49}{32} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of paint used is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{32}\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231327 :question/id #uuid "397919cc-d741-5cda-b965-6c580266770f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21973" :question/problem "$9x - 4x =$" :question/worked-solution "We have two like terms, $9x$ and $-4x$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term by subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 4x &=  \\\\\n(9 - 4)x &=  \\\\\n&= 5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231328 :question/id #uuid "cc542e1d-b768-5c39-9e88-1f60f3e31e6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21974" :question/problem "$6a - 2a + 5a + 3 =$" :question/worked-solution "We have three like terms, $6a$, $-2a$, and $5a$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6a - 2a + 5a + 3 &=  \\\\\n(6 - 2 + 5)a + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 9a + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231331 :question/id #uuid "1333e06d-19a6-5862-b8f5-6d82b8131a04" :question/math-academy-id "e-21977" :question/problem "$(7 + 4y)\\cdot 6=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(7 + 4y) \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 6 + 4y \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 42 + 24y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231332 :question/id #uuid "7da54966-8a23-5849-8f23-4a4a43441719" :question/math-academy-id "e-21978" :question/problem "$4(3a + 7b)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(3a + 7b) &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 3a + 4 \\cdot 7b &=  \\\\\n&= 12a + 28b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231333 :question/id #uuid "a05a2775-0dfc-500a-93b3-808df6aba208" :question/math-academy-id "e-21979" :question/problem "$(9x - 4)\\cdot 5=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x - 4) \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n9x \\cdot 5 - 4 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 45x - 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231334 :question/id #uuid "1ec9bb9c-6470-573e-bc3b-b2ffe110b9e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21980" :question/problem "$6(2p - 5q)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6(2p - 5q) &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2p - 6 \\cdot 5q &=  \\\\\n&= 12p - 30q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231335 :question/id #uuid "e21115ff-5dbd-5e50-a0b4-d6d76cd23031" :question/math-academy-id "e-21986" :question/problem "$$\n4x^2y'' + 3x(x+2)y' - 2y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(1-2n)}{f(n)}\\,a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x+2){y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -2y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as a power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x+2){y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -2y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ 2(2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n))+3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-2{a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4(\\lambda +n)^{2}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-2{a}_{n}+3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} & = \\\\ 2{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(2(\\lambda +n)^{2}+(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+\\frac{3}{2}(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2(2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(2(\\lambda +n)^{2}+(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+\\frac{3}{2}(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(2(\\lambda +n)^{2}+(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+\\frac{3}{2}(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we take $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for $n\\geq1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2(\\lambda +n)^{2}+(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+\\frac{3}{2}(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ ((\\lambda +n)+1)(2(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\lambda=\\dfrac12$ and solving for $a_n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((\\frac{1}{2}+n)+1)(2(\\frac{1}{2}+n)-1){a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+n-1){a}_{n-1} \\\\ (n+\\frac{3}{2})(2n){a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3}{2}(n-\\frac{1}{2}){a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(2n+3){a}_{n} & =\\frac{3}{4}(1-2n){a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{3(1-2n)}{4n(2n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$f(n) = 4n(2n+3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231344 :question/id #uuid "692b61e4-432e-5984-a7dc-4d601d770e42" :question/math-academy-id "e-21996" :question/problem "$8b+5+3(4b-6)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8b + 5 + 3(4b - 6) &=  \\\\\n8b + 5 + 3 \\cdot 4b - 3 \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 8b + 5 + 12b - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting expression has two groups like terms, $8b$ and $12b$, and $5$ and $-18$. Combining the terms in each group, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8b + 5 + 12b - 18 &=  \\\\\n8b + 12b + 5 - 18 &=  \\\\\n(8 + 12)b + (5 - 18) &=  \\\\\n&= 20b - 13 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(20b,13)=1\n$$\n\nthere is nothing to factor out.\n\n$$\n20b - 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression is equivalent to $20b-13$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231383 :question/id #uuid "c537983b-01c2-5bd3-92cd-52a642114128" :question/math-academy-id "e-23330" :question/problem "How many feet are in $216$ inches?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the unit conversion between feet and inches:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{ft} = 12 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from inches to feet, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\,\\textrm{ft} = 1 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $216$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $216{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n216 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft &= 216 \\cdot 1in \\\\\n\\frac{216}{12}ft &= 216in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{216}{12}\n$$\n\nas the division $216 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  |  | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $12$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  |  | $\\phantom{0}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $216\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $18\\,\\textrm{ft}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231384 :question/id #uuid "4d70b5d5-d67d-546c-bd3f-04c6dca4822d" :question/math-academy-id "e-23331" :question/problem "How many yards are in $408$ inches?" :question/worked-solution "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$$408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$$34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c27ebb29-4694-5fbe-96ad-328729ff0ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = - (6a_{n - 1})/((n + 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230347} {:answer/id #uuid "c52830d1-9054-5ae2-9f66-e8d1ad54bcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = - (6a_{n - 1})/(n(3n + 2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230348} {:answer/id #uuid "cec2dfef-dc32-5168-a647-358cfd09a0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} =-\\frac{6a_{n - 1}}{3n + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230349} {:answer/id #uuid "33aa076d-c46b-5ab9-a25f-3e10a4573cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = - (6a_{n - 1})/(n(3n - 2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230350} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f0de8-c1e7-578b-bf17-a330bf1cadea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = - (6a_{n - 1})/(n(n + 2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230351}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d6cc35de-6b8c-5c52-a971-678d75709be0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc034d2-4573-5642-aa1a-7eb4549bab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{n(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377367} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb6a3bc-f18b-5097-8d46-457248965990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{3n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377368} {:answer/id #uuid "1708db22-efe4-5552-b871-f3fa58002e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{n(3n-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377369} {:answer/id #uuid "2426a4f5-125d-5dba-a918-3af2dcb718ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{n(3n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377370} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a755d-9247-5bbf-867f-b711f7dd10c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{(n+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2426a4f5-125d-5dba-a918-3af2dcb718ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{n(3n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377370} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3c3b26-7548-5161-ad6e-b84801efd465" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac03556-cde0-5e3a-bd80-5d077ee0ac42" :question/math-academy-id "q-344008" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,….$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 6y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 6y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (3{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(3(\\lambda +n)^{2}+2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(3(\\lambda +n)^{2}+2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(3(\\lambda +n)^{2}+2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ we take $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of ${x}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(\\lambda +n)^{2}+2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+6{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda =0$ into the above and solve for ${a}_{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+n)(3(0+n)+2){a}_{n} & =-6{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{6{a}_{n-1}}{n(3n+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ad0782-5823-5282-aede-f86aca38346f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(6n - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230353} {:answer/id #uuid "b58166d6-e1c3-591b-8888-ee47c5a37f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(6n - 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230354} {:answer/id #uuid "04a4f4c4-af74-5a0d-bab8-1042331825b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = (n + 1)(6n + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230355} {:answer/id #uuid "1fdc4a8e-4de2-5b98-9d39-e8cd0ae0b788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(6n + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230356} {:answer/id #uuid "620e098e-4df7-53b2-ad22-679c10f48a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(6n + 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230357}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6c891d3e-7c46-552c-bae0-e022e653f374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f62ad5a-974e-5fb2-be10-e1840e429650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=n(6n-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377373} {:answer/id #uuid "22674148-39c6-5a80-a7f1-c959c9b67c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=n(6n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377374} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa438a8-f09e-5f98-9297-a7ed364a607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=n(6n+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377375} {:answer/id #uuid "6edfb666-38f8-517e-8bcc-0848bac78a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=(n+1)(6n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377376} {:answer/id #uuid "2255bd0a-e269-5269-a19e-33c532b6be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=n(6n-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fa438a8-f09e-5f98-9297-a7ed364a607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=n(6n+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377375} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c61b0c-afc2-5256-a299-e20927de2505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf735217-6eda-5729-a45a-f6c7f5ac0bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344011" :question/problem "$$\n3{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′}+(2x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda \\in (0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{f(n)},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (3{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+\\frac{5}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)-2){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)-2){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)-2){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -2)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda \\in (0,1),$ we take $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of ${x}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)-2){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(3(\\lambda +n)-2)(2(\\lambda +n)+1){a}_{n}+2{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}$ into the above and solve for ${a}_{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(3(\\frac{2}{3}+n)-2)(2(\\frac{2}{3}+n)+1){a}_{n} & =-2{a}_{n-1} \\\\ 3n(2n+\\frac{7}{3}){a}_{n} & =-4{a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(6n+7){a}_{n} & =-4{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{n(6n+7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=n(6n+7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262707e8-a17c-59ce-971e-969d38b738cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545282} {:answer/id #uuid "5239f06a-9c88-5796-bad4-dd01e0598425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545351} {:answer/id #uuid "80d1efc3-9f36-58cd-8661-fa04801b6e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545437} {:answer/id #uuid "828b3fdb-edff-5b38-88e4-936da5ad6e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545444} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf722e-820e-5637-ba90-106a58a4e7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf722e-820e-5637-ba90-106a58a4e7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545484} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fc702b-edd1-54aa-8ad1-69534578dde9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5277b0dd-a1ab-523e-8996-74d7092ca199" :question/math-academy-id "q-103615" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{18x-6}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18x-6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18x}{3}-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot x-2 & = \\\\ 6x-2. & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233102 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e02fccab-e1b2-5ffe-aac2-770db93da3fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107542" :question/problem "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2,{x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $3.$ Find $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-2))(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x+2)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+2)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}-x(1+2\\text{i})-x(1-2\\text{i})+(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}-2x+5) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}-4x+5x+10) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+x+10) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}+3x+30 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+3x+30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1814fbcd-9f92-515e-9828-c49eef2bbaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574987} {:answer/id #uuid "4be48e3e-1c40-52d4-a265-9d0205cf8f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575078} {:answer/id #uuid "5731e481-0681-52ab-b6b9-e017c2f03b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575102} {:answer/id #uuid "69f8f087-b4ab-539a-a92a-71c2b7e860e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575138} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4a93a5-5f1b-534c-a5dc-2740ee32a078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f8f087-b4ab-539a-a92a-71c2b7e860e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575138} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0bdd98-1734-5283-bd19-3a50cb6e8817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e08294c6-a979-573d-8467-4eac7fae6a3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111362" :question/problem "Given that  $(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2)=3{s}^{4}+a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+cs+8,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(2s)({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+9{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+4s+4{s}^{2}+12s+8 & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+11{s}^{3}+16{s}^{2}+16s+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ $b=16,$ and $c=16.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=11+16+16=43.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233164 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "424cb02e-6abe-5b0a-a3a1-0d282095bf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-113208" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $5w,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-5+w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2w+w)+(-5+7)}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w+2}{5w} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233165 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7923339-edc9-53aa-b1a3-e887cd661492" :question/math-academy-id "q-113228" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3}{2z}=p$ and $\\frac{2}{z}=q,$ what is $p-q?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\np-q=\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2z$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{4}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-4}{2z} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233166 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9310867-9c9d-5cda-82b8-65958a0a6cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113229" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{7}{5d}=g$ and $\\frac{2f}{d}=h,$ what is $g-h?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\ng-h=\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5d$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{10f}{5d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7-10f}{5d} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233167 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b38716ee-f3bb-5700-84fa-b220f9e8f36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113302" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $4v(1+w)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w}{4v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233168 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b32f30a0-1d44-58db-add6-c8f422294b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113308" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $(t-3)(u+4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $t-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{t-3}{t-3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233169 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91080612-a13e-542e-a71f-3d6e5763c823" :question/math-academy-id "q-113311" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $n(m-4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n\\cdot n}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} {:answer/id #uuid "62de7813-7886-5bed-86cd-1ca23e600b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}^{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513799} {:answer/id #uuid "65428549-ccd7-5201-8026-265c549f0352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513804} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af8b6-1444-5218-bbb1-bf6a887d157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{19}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513863} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf69104-089b-5eec-8f81-3bf53d5632ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} :answer-field/id #uuid "6982cbcb-0bd9-554d-8104-e02dd398a383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113665" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} & = \\\\ {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7949e7bf-09d1-5ce9-ac3c-0d7f26b2e6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113670" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} & = \\\\ {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233176 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353f4779-4251-5a93-b99f-1eda6757b24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113674" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233177 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eab1e18-d0df-58e6-917b-50a1d74ed3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113680" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{(\\frac{8}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233178 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63703f37-ab84-53aa-922b-b2e419a9f1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113695" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{(\\frac{7}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233187 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75adb720-6eca-5ea7-923e-0df2cd4a26f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113791" :question/problem "$\\frac{3t}{2}÷\\frac{u}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3t}{2}÷\\frac{u}{5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t}{2}÷\\frac{u}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{u} & = \\\\ \\frac{15t}{2u} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233188 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f25be88-1ba1-5296-9bf3-46da3b9bc580" :question/math-academy-id "q-113795" :question/problem "$\\frac{r}{7}÷\\frac{2}{3s}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{7}÷\\frac{2}{3s},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{7}÷\\frac{2}{3s} & = \\\\ \\frac{r}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3s}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3rs}{14} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620efcf8-fe45-5654-a7f3-88db5fb39541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(w+2)}{7{w}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538451} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dc84d0-2123-5c46-be45-9dca7733c77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7w}{3(w+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538701} {:answer/id #uuid "ca928d1f-961b-583d-a769-be5f671d1b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14{w}^{2}}{w+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538705} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14w}{3(w+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538732} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1bc848-a943-502b-beea-11031d924356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{w+2}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14w}{3(w+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ebb594e-17b9-5253-8215-8996f4a7ec24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97895caf-82df-5b95-8916-1e87ffb09dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113809" :question/problem "$\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}÷\\frac{w(w+2)}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{w}^{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{w(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14{w}^{2}}{3w(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14{w}^{\\cancel{2}}}{3\\cancel{w}(w+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{14w}{3(w+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we canceled out a common factor of $w$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233190 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbea9a41-f5e4-5e72-8db5-63fafb728c0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113811" :question/problem "$\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}÷\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}÷\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q-2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}÷\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}\\cdot \\frac{q-2}{{q}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{{q}^{3}}}{q+2}\\cdot \\frac{q-2}{\\cancel{{q}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{q-2}{q+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of ${q}^{3}$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233191 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c634461e-ebd8-518e-86e3-78052b8ff842" :question/math-academy-id "q-113816" :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\frac{3}{5{z}^{2}}$ by $\\frac{4}{{z}^{5}}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5{z}^{2}}÷\\frac{4}{{z}^{5}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5{z}^{2}}÷\\frac{4}{{z}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{5{z}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{5}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{z}^{5}}{20{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{z}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{2}}{20{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{z}^{3}\\cdot \\cancel{{z}^{2}}}{20\\cancel{{z}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{z}^{3}}{20} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of ${z}^{2}$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233192 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d25096f9-3bbc-5f50-945a-370b7541ee46" :question/math-academy-id "q-113819" :question/problem "$\\frac{3rs}{7t}÷\\frac{r}{7st}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3rs}{7t}÷\\frac{r}{7st},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3rs}{7t}÷\\frac{r}{7st} & = \\\\ \\frac{3rs}{7t}\\cdot \\frac{7st}{r} & = \\\\ \\frac{3s\\cdot r}{7t}\\cdot \\frac{7t\\cdot s}{r} & = \\\\ \\frac{3s\\cdot \\cancel{r}}{\\cancel{7t}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{7t}\\cdot s}{\\cancel{r}} & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233203 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2db7e751-8996-5e49-85c9-d87fd5e920f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114085" :question/problem "If $\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},$ then what is $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{4}}{14{p}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{2\\cdot 5{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{3}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot 7{p}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7{p}^{2}}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5{r}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5{r}^{3}}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot \\cancel{7{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{r}{2{p}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{r}{4{p}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233204 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b0b1db2-64c3-5117-b282-677fcdef7c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-114088" :question/problem "If $\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},$ then what is $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{18{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(p+5)^{2}\\cdot (p+5)}{2{w}^{2}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot 2{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}\\cdot (p+5)}{\\cancel{2{w}^{2}}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot \\cancel{2{w}^{2}}}{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(p+5)}{2w}\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ \\frac{9(p+5)}{2w}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0253d148-6cfe-5c62-a23b-66b9dbd84f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=2,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519998} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4ed9a6-0960-541a-a947-eae413b4c345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=\\sqrt{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520062} {:answer/id #uuid "510e6b93-921f-54a3-aaf4-f17810afc277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=-3,\\,\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520114} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b976e5-3d11-5eb8-b26a-10408be52ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=\\sqrt{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520295} {:answer/id #uuid "de8f6f1e-2d9f-5d34-897a-08a567c8845d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8f6f1e-2d9f-5d34-897a-08a567c8845d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520313} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fde94e-983d-5d42-9eeb-795ef8488a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "978fe695-4870-5063-84c2-77d196d63866" :question/math-academy-id "q-114145" :question/problem "Solve ${m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}-3{m}^{2})-(2m-6) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m-3)-2(m-3) & = \\\\ (m-3)\\cdot {m}^{2}-(m-3)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (m-3)({m}^{2}-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m-3)({m}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-3 & =0 \\\\ m & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({m}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2} & =2 \\\\ m & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233208 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75362855-fd0b-5b7a-9b8c-e751dd6f31c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114205" :question/problem "Solve $-18{v}^{3}-63{v}^{2}+2v+7=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7 & = \\\\ 9{v}^{2}(2v+7)-1(2v+7) & = \\\\ (9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{v}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2v+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v & =-7 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $v=-\\frac{7}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233210 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34d06e7f-3dec-5eb5-bc4c-37dfc5e1ecc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114210" :question/problem "Solve $-{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}+25m+75=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}+3{m}^{2})+(-25m-75) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m+3)-25(m+3) & = \\\\ ({m}^{2}-25)(m+3) & = \\\\ (m+5)(m-5)(m+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m+5)(m-5)(m+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=-3,\\,\\pm 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520052} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4f5de1-9dc9-522b-8d92-8e06bd8036e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520053} {:answer/id #uuid "3dda4960-6f22-50d0-9a1b-0aa60ac485af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=-4,\\,\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520079} {:answer/id #uuid "79fdb319-1ee7-52a8-9cfa-8b3ba59ff030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520167} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b17d87-d9ba-516d-a315-6c82fb45de3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520052} :answer-field/id #uuid "888e6ecc-ed2b-57e9-9b31-50cf1eb43d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4ef63b9-4994-5aab-8c0b-2be94f67268a" :question/math-academy-id "q-114339" :question/problem "Solve $8{u}^{3}-6{u}^{2}-72u+54=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27 & = \\\\ (4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2})-(36u-27) & = \\\\ {u}^{2}(4u-3)-9(4u-3) & = \\\\ (4u-3)\\cdot {u}^{2}-(4u-3)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (4u-3)({u}^{2}-9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(4u-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-3 & =0 \\\\ 4u & =3 \\\\ u & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({u}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2} & =9 \\\\ u & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233430 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5bbefea1-ce92-5926-bfeb-0943ee329e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-134565" :question/problem "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{40} & =\\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5}=2\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2\\sqrt[3]{5}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2+14)\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233445 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29d1acd9-50dd-50a1-81b1-6ea0f409001f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14077" :question/problem "Solve ${x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2})-(3x+21) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+7)-3(x+7) & = \\\\ (x+7)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(x+7)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x+7)({x}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x+7)({x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =0 \\\\ x & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-7,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233447 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89a4870e-3b2b-5850-829c-07e057bc3b5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14102" :question/problem "Solve $6{y}^{3}+4=8{y}^{2}+3y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract $8{y}^{2}$ and $3y$ from both sides to make the right side $0.$ To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4 & = \\\\ (6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2})-(3y-4) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(3y-4)-(3y-4) & = \\\\ (3y-4)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(3y-4)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (3y-4)(2{y}^{2}-1) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(3y-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-4 & =0 \\\\ 3y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233448 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c46d24cd-9dc5-57b1-ae1d-738d211cd23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14108" :question/problem "Solve $14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3 & = \\\\ (14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2})-(6x-3) & = \\\\ 7{x}^{2}(2x-1)-3(2x-1) & = \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot 7{x}^{2}-(2x-1)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(7{x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233450 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf73d1fb-d00b-52e1-a6f6-cf33f3e2cc86" :question/math-academy-id "q-14121" :question/problem "Find all the real solutions to the equation $3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9 & = \\\\ (3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2})+(27z-9) & = \\\\ {z}^{2}(3z-1)+9(3z-1) & = \\\\ (3z-1)\\cdot {z}^{2}+(3z-1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (3z-1)({z}^{2}+9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3z-1)({z}^{2}+9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3z-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-1 & =0 \\\\ 3z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({z}^{2}+9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${z}^{2}+9=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $z=\\frac{1}{3}$ is the only real solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233457 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e14cd639-77cd-5b3f-8bb3-3533906531ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-141474" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{18{v}^{7}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $18{v}^{7}$ as the product $9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v$ which contains a perfect square $(9{v}^{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{18{v}^{7}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}\\cdot 2v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ |3{v}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |{v}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ 3|v{|}^{3}\\sqrt{2v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e017c6d-4e9c-5cb6-a364-f311476d6242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a718be7-eb73-54f8-925e-728120665d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-143041" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233497 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85662b87-da58-5626-af7a-d96298ab6e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14440" :question/problem "$\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-12x+32} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+10)(x-8)}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)(x-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+10)\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-5)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-8)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233499 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a64202d0-b55e-542f-b377-4f81e0330d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-14461" :question/problem "$\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12} & = \\\\ \\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}{28x-35} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(4x-5)}{3x-6}\\cdot \\frac{(3x-6)(x-2)}{7(4x-5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cancel{(4x-5)}}{\\cancel{(3x-6)}}\\cdot \\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(3x-6)}}{7\\cancel{(4x-5)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-2)}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4x-8}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23032e7c-326e-5437-9937-76f3a526071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-50x+600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23032e7c-326e-5437-9937-76f3a526071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-50x+600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232252} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b055d5b-a5f6-5237-8160-759c230d4419" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3801e84-afaa-5faf-b271-995c9174951f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167196" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of couches the company should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$ The required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$12\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(12{x}^{2},600x,7\\,200)=12.\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200 & = \\\\ 12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600) & = \\\\ 12({x}^{2}-50x+600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-50x+600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d6f845-951b-5e88-86b4-5c6cb72f54c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d6f845-951b-5e88-86b4-5c6cb72f54c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232268} :answer-field/id #uuid "867ecee0-07f2-54a4-b55f-fb546d4b264d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91eb1d3c-008d-5a1b-b2ee-5b1886437bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-167212" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of cakes the bakery should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$ The required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$4\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(4{x}^{2},320x,6\\,000)=4\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000 & = \\\\ 4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500) & = \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d120728-cd1a-5fb3-837e-5e6b7b70b38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-30x+200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d120728-cd1a-5fb3-837e-5e6b7b70b38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-30x+200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b0b7f3-bcd5-5ef0-9241-064c93983260" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02bf8797-4e8d-58c0-a3b6-dde491cb5f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167228" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company should produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$ The required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-90x+600=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$3\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $3{x}^{2}-90x+600$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(3{x}^{2},90x,600)=3\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-90x+600 & = \\\\ 3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-30x+200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-30x+200.$"}]}
